  "assumptions": [
    "Illustration: a made-up two-setting loss bowl with simulated batch noise, not a real model trained on real data.",
    "Loss = (3x² + z²)/2, drawn at half its value as a surface with contours. It is steeper along x, so overshoot shows there first. Without noise, x swings grow once learning rate × 3 > 2, so for rates above about 0.67.",
    "Noise is Gaussian and centred on the true slope. Its spread is 1.5·√((64−B)/(63·B)) for batch size B, as when sampling B of 64 points, so it is exactly 0 at 64. Noise values come from the fixed seed, the run number and the step number.",
    "The orange arrow is the next mini-batch step (−rate × noisy slope), the way the ball will move. The grey arrow is the full-data step (−rate × true slope). Both are drawn at their true length in bowl units (scale 1).",
    "A run ends after 40 steps (finished), or when the loss goes above 8, the bowl's corner value (diverged). The trail holds the start point plus one point per step, so up to 41 points.",
    "The ball is drawn on the bowl, and paths are capped at the bowl's top. The loss readout always shows the true value. A step lowers the loss only on average."
  ]
}